WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.437
Here's why this matters.

2
00:00:01.437 --> 00:00:05.038
A certificate is a signed claim
that's already been handed out.

3
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.972
It sits on devices, smart cards,

4
00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:11.074
load balancers and trust stores
nobody remembers configuring.

5
00:00:11.074 --> 00:00:13.475
It doesn't move when the directory moves.

6
00:00:13.475 --> 00:00:17.850
It keeps working until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

7
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.051
AD CS is the hardest part of Active Directory
to leave: it's wired into the forest,

8
00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:26.572
the cloud replacements cover
only part of the estate,

9
00:00:26.572 --> 00:00:30.813
and domain controllers need
certificates as long as they exist.

10
00:00:30.813 --> 00:00:34.423
Meanwhile the rules keep moving,
from shrinking public TLS

11
00:00:34.423 --> 00:00:38.549
lifetimes to strong-mapping
enforcement to post-quantum planning.

12
00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:42.523
So this runs as its own program,
because it fails at expiry,

13
00:00:42.523 --> 00:00:46.460
not at cutover, often months
later and far from the cause.
